WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Funchal Airport, on the island of Madeira,
is one of the most difficult places

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.480
in Europe to land a commercial plane.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.400
It wasn't easy to build a runway here
back in the 1960s.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.440
Madeira is basically one huge mountain
just sticking out of the Atlantic Ocean.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.080
There isn't any part of this island

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.640
flat enough for long enough
that it can support a runway.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.920
But with a lot of earthmoving,
it was possible for them to build

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.840
one short runway, enough for the
relatively lightweight aircraft of the day.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.320
And then European tourism and
the package holiday boom started,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.920
and planes
kept getting bigger.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.600
The runway was extended another 200 meters
in the '70s, but it still wasn't enough.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.560
There was nowhere to extend to.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.800
There was another bit of cliff,
a few hundred meters to the east,

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.560
that could technically be flattened.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.840
But between there and the existing runway,
there was nothing but air.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.800
The only option was to build a bridge:

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.480
big enough, wide enough and
strong enough, somehow,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.720
to handle a fully loaded
airliner landing on it.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.840
Basically:
to put most of the runway up on stilts.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.920
So that's what they did.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.120
IN PORTUGUESE:
 
 

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.400
It's only when you get

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.440
underneath the runway and stand up close
that you realise the sheer scale of this.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.600
It's unlike any other space
I've ever been in.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.800
There's a whole boatyard under here,
a go kart track.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.720
The noise you can hear is industry.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.360
There's more than 30 metres
between each of these pillars.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.320
The tallest ones are more than
50 metres high.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.520
Just the base of each pillar is taller
than I am.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.680
The foundations go
far down into the ground.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.280
This whole area had to be flattened
and stabilised

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.080
so it could stand up
to the weight.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.840
On its own, a runway on stilts
shouldn't make a landing more difficult.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.160
A runway is a runway, right?

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.920
The difficulty comes from
what's around and under it.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.560
The mountains cause fast gusting winds.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.160
And the approach has a late, low turn
that requires special training.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.040
And pilots aren't even allowed to start
that training until they've had 200 hours

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.320
flying the type of aircraft
they'll be landing here.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.640
In the airport documentation,

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.000
there's a line that says pilots should
watch the wind indicators on the runway

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.480
because occasionally, "they will indicate
wind from opposite directions".

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.440
To be clear, this is safe.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.440
There hasn't been an incident
on this runway in nearly half a century.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.400
For most passengers, this is just a normal
landing at a normal airport.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.800
Or at worst,
a bit of a bumpy, turbulent landing.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.160
And the reason for that is

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.880
a lot of pilot training
and some very big civil engineering.

